Relevant columns and why they matter  
• Person.PID ‒ unique identifier for every actor/actress; needed all the time.  
• Person.Name ‒ lets us find Shahrukh Khan (“%Shahrukh%”, “%Shah Rukh%”).  
• M_Cast.PID ‒ connects an actor to a film.  
• M_Cast.MID ‒ connects a film to all its actors; used to jump from one actor to all co‑actors in the same movie.  

Main conditions that will repeatedly appear  
• Person.Name LIKE '%Shahrukh%'  OR Person.Name LIKE '%Shah Rukh%'  → Shahrukh Khan himself  
• c1.MID = c2.MID  (self–join on M_Cast) → everybody who shares a movie.  
• Exclude the first‑degree set and Shahrukh himself when looking for Shahrukh‑number 2.  


Exploration & solution in 10 incremental queries
```sql
-- 1) Find the exact PID(s) that belong to Shahrukh Khan
SELECT  "PID", "Name"
FROM    "Person"
WHERE   "Name" LIKE '%Shahrukh%' OR "Name" LIKE '%Shah Rukh%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List all movie‑ids in which Shahrukh Khan acted
SELECT  DISTINCT "MID"
FROM    "M_Cast"
WHERE   "PID" IN (SELECT "PID"
                  FROM   "Person"
                  WHERE  "Name" LIKE '%Shahrukh%' OR "Name" LIKE '%Shah Rukh%')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Every actor who ever appeared with Shahrukh (Shahrukh‑number = 1)
SELECT  DISTINCT c2."PID"
FROM    "M_Cast" AS c1
JOIN    "M_Cast" AS c2 ON c1."MID" = c2."MID"
WHERE   c1."PID" IN (SELECT "PID"
                     FROM   "Person"
                     WHERE  "Name" LIKE '%Shahrukh%' OR "Name" LIKE '%Shah Rukh%')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
